story. Stock-based compensation of $20K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["OPAL Fuels Inc. is a micro-cap industrials business with a $130M market cap on $349M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +14% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 10% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The company is burning through $34M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 11.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 0.3\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 27% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.2\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.6) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (15.1% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries a 60% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.6047021469609919},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries more leverage than most Industrials 